== Date ==
This class is deprecated after Java 1.1
Works with longs (milliseconds after december 31st 1969)
It's a bad option because can't offer good presentation of dates and internationalization of time zone

== Calendar ==
Isn't deprecated, but obsolete, more used in legacy systems
Calendar is an abstract class, if you want to instantiate this, create an object of any of his subclasses (GregorianCalendar, BuddhistCalendar)

=== java.time ===
+ Added after Java 8
+ Immutable
+ Don't need to parse everytime
+ MIN and MAX
+ You can do more things using referringClass.get(ChronoField.ENUM)
+ Characters P/T/S/H are from the ISO 8601 to represent time

== LocalDate ==
+ No limit for dates
+ Use the pattern of the databases

== LocalTime ==
+ Works like LocalDate

== LocalDateTime ==
+ Works like the others above
+ Can be parsed with LocalDate.atTime(LocalTime or time) or LocalTime.atDate(LocalDate or date)

== Instant ==
+ A way to get the zulu / UTC time (good for internationalization)
+ Nano seconds

== Duration ==
+ A way to get the difference between two dates, based on hours, minutes, seconds, nanoseconds]
+ LocalDateTimes/LocalTimes classes
+ Can't get between two LocalDates, because this class don't have the unit seconds
+ Works with hours, minutes and seconds
+ Can convert hours to days, months to years etc.

== Period ==
+ Like the class Duration, but based on years, months and days

== ChronoUnit ==
+ A good way to convert values and compare dates (method between())

== ChronoField ==
+ Convert fields of dates

== TemporalAdjusters & TemporalAdjuster ==
+ TemporalAdjusters (class with created util methods)
+ TemporalAdjuster (classes which implements TemporalAdjuster can create logics to manipulate dates) -> TemporalAdjusterTest01.java
+ With this you can manipulate dates, like: "LocalDate.with(new GetNextBusinessDay() (created) or TemporalAdjusters.firstDayOfNextMonth())"

== Zones ==
+ Can convert times to another time zones (ZonedId and ZonedDateTime)
+ Can create new time zones with ZoneOffset and use with OffsetDateTime/OffsetTime

== Chronology ==
+ You can convert dates between them using like: "JapaneseDate.from(LocalDate.now)"
- ISO -> LocalDate, LocalDateTime, etc.
- JapaneseDate
- Thai BuddhistDate
- HijrahDate
- MinguoDate
